### Page 2 of 4

```text
Planning Commission
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
May 23, 2024
PZ-24-00041 PC Meeting Packet 5.23.24
PZ-24-00041 Agency Comments Rev #2
PZ-24-00041 Public Comment E. Rabel 5.21.24
PZ-24-00041 Public Comment J. Deisch 5.21.24
PZ-24-00041 Public Comment Lisa A. Bennett 5.22.24
Attachments:
Cate Cundall, Associate Planner, said that AVI, PC , on behalf of Cowboy Kids Land
Co., LLC, submitted a Preliminary Development Plan application for Cowboy Kids at
Ridley Ranch located southwest of Interstate 25 and Ridley Road (County Road 220).
Originally submitted as a 45 lot subdivision that included 2 non-buildable open space
lots it has been revised and approximately 50 acres of what was open space is now
proposed as a buildable lot.
Ms. Cundall said that the subdivision would be accessed via three approaches, two of
which will be from the I-25 service road and one via Ridley Road. A preliminary traffic
study has been submitted with the application. Future development of any lot within the
development for a commercial use will have to go through a planning action process
that will allow for the public to make comment.  Agency review comments included
gross lot sizes, access locations, and unresolved plat notes. Several written public
comments in opposition to the development have been received in response to the
public notice.
Rebekha Dostal from the Laramie County Attorney's office was present to answer
questions regarding the project. Ms. Dostal said the company's status with the
Secretary of State is now current but moving forward on any future subdivision or site
plan applications the applicant will be required to provide a Certificate of Authority for
whoever is signing the application.
Chairman Caughey opened the Public Hearing. Speaking in opposition of the project
were: Steve Cooke, Eric Lundgren, Troy Guimond, Jeff Barnes, Cathy Barnes,
Raeann Merry, Chad Wilson, and Russell Henning. Concerns included well
interference, averaging gross acres for lot sizes, not labeling commercial and light
industrial lots, reconfiguration of proposal is not similar to what was in the neighbor
letter so public can't properly comment, commercial sites aren't addressed for water
useage, light and noise pollution from commercial lots, and so many changes should
require a new application.
Hearing no further comments the Public Hearing was closed. Justin Arnold answered
the question on open space. If designated in a cluster development those open space
lots become un-buildable and when applicant is averaging gross acres any future
dividing of platted lots is not allowed.
Mark Christensen, applicants agent, said that the PDP is now 44 lots averaging 6.32
acres since the one open space was removed from the PDP. He said that the
purpose of the PDP was to identify problems and that the subdivision application
would address agency comments as they move forward. Mr. Christensen responded
Page 2 of 4
```

### Page 3 of 4

```text
Planning Commission
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
May 23, 2024
to the question of what open range was removed, why the lots targeted for commercial
and light industrial were not labeled, easements within the subdivision, and calculating
gross acres using R-O-W.
Chairman Caughey said Mr. Christensen addressed public comments on access and
the septic, but that obviously it looks like the original application was pieced it
together to come up with this current application. There are significant inconsistencies
in there. Moving forward, if it moves forward, what is the plan to make sure those
aren't continued to be hiccups? Mr. Christensen said that this was just a PDP. The
subdivision application is more strenuous and technical so they would unsure they are
checking all those boxes. Some components were carried forward from the last
application but they have ensured that they have corrected all of those for the
subdivision permit.
A motion was made by Member Gaspar, seconded by Member Smith, that
this agenda item be postponed until there is a complete PDP application.
Vote 3-0.
Caughey, Gaspar, Smith
Aye:
Stadel, Watkins
Excused:
Reports
Justin Arnold, Planning Manager, announced that Planning is tasked with  enforcing the codified
regulations and that applications meets those criteria for a development. He would like the public
to know that currently planning staff has been working for about seven or eight months now
rewriting the Land Use Regulations to make them more efficient and understandable for the
general public and justifiable. We are tasked with respecting private property rights while
ensuring that the development is responsible and done correctly. I would note that in the coming
months, probably three or four, we will be going through reviews. We will have legal reviews of
those proposal drafts and that will be available for public comments. I certainly would appreciate
all input from local businesses, the public, anybody who is a stakeholder within our jurisdiction to
provide their input cause that's what we are here for. We're all here to agree upon how we are
going to responsibly develop our properties for later generations. I would appreciate that when we
get to that stage. Grammatical checks are being completed and as soon as there is a clean draft
it will be going to the County Attorney for a legal review but it will be put out for stakeholder input.
There will Google forms on the Planning and Development website so we can get your feedback
for the duration of the process which he anticipates will be several months. Mr. Arnold is hoping
for a good regulation that will be good for a decade or so.
